<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fatali Khan Khoyski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fatali Khan Khoyski was an Azerbaijani lawyer and politician who served as the first prime minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> Democratic Republic from May 1918 to 1919. His governments moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, built state institutions and defended the republic's independence. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> overthrew the republic in 1920, he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, where an Armenian nationalist assassinated him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fatali-khan-khoyski.jpg" alt="Fatali Khan Khoyski" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ателье Буллы · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fatali_Khan_Khoyski.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fatali Khan Khoyski was born on 7 December 1875 (O.S. 25 November 1875) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaki-azerbaijan">Nukha</a>, now Shaki, into the noble family of Iskandar Khan Khoyski, a colonel in the Russian Army. His great-grandfather Jafar Qoli, the Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoy">Khoy</a>, sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the Russo-Persian war of 1804 to 1813 and was rewarded by Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> with the Khanate of Shaki. Khoyski graduated from the Ganja Classical Gymnasium in 1893, where he learned Russian, Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, French and German, and from the Law Faculty of Moscow University in 1901, and worked as a court lawyer in Ganja, Sukhumi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batumi">Batumi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected to the Second State Duma from Elizavetpol Governorate, he criticised Russian colonial policy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in a speech on 2 February 1907. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917 he joined the committee of the Muslim National Councils and was elected chairman of the Baku City Duma, regarded as the chief rival of the Baku Soviet. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musavat">Musavat</a> summit of October 1917 he spoke for Azerbaijan's autonomy, and in December he was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transcaucasian-sejm">Transcaucasian Sejm</a> and named Minister of Justice of the Transcaucasian Democratic Federative Republic.</p><p>When the Azerbaijan Democratic Republic was proclaimed on 28 May 1918 he formed its first cabinet and on 30 May sent a radiogram announcing independence to the world's political centres. His governments worked for an alliance with the Turkish government, helped remove the Centrocaspian Dictatorship from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> and set up diplomatic ties; he sought recognition of independence at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a>. Under them Turkish was made the official language, education was nationalised, the first steps toward a national army were taken, a multi-party system and the manat currency were introduced, and Azerbaijan State University was founded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In January 1920 Georgy Chicherin repeatedly asked Khoyski to open a front against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-denikin">Anton Denikin</a>; he refused, saying the republic would not meddle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s internal affairs. He moved his family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> on 28 April 1920.</p><p>On 19 June 1920 Aram Yerganian shot him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> near Erivansky Square, as part of Operation Nemesis organised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-revolutionary-federation">Armenian Revolutionary Federation</a>, which said he had played a major role in the September 1918 massacre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>. He was buried in Tiflis next to Mirza Fatali Akhundzade, in a ceremony arranged by the Persian consulate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fatali Khan Khoyski” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic was led by the Musavat party under Mammad Amin Rasulzade and prime ministers such as <b>Fatali Khan Khoyski</b>, with a multi-party parliament that included minority representatives.</blockquote><a href="/state/azerbaijan-democratic-republic">Azerbaijan Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaki-azerbaijan">Shaki</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.19,47.17&amp;year=1904" aria-label="Shaki on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5703 694 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Fatali Khan Khoyski"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6153 964h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6153 964h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.19,47.17&amp;year=1904">What was here in 1904? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
